 {
  "id": "M34/concept1",
  "lesson": "M34",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does attention divide the scores q . k by sqrt(d)?",
  "answer": "If the entries of q and k are independent with mean 0 and variance 1, then q . k is a sum of d independent terms of variance 1, so its variance is d and its typical size sqrt(d). Without the division the scores grow with the width, the softmax puts almost all its weight on one key, and its Jacobian diag(p) - p p^T nearly vanishes, so little gradient flows. Dividing by sqrt(d) keeps the scores at unit variance at every width.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
